.TH SENSOR-ONBOARD 1 "September 2026" "sensor-control" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sensor-onboard \- read onboard Raspberry Pi sensors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sensor-onboard
.I identify
.br
.B sensor-onboard
.IR list | mock | setup | enable | version | \-\-version | \-v
.br
.B sensor-onboard
[\fIsensor\fR|\fIall\fR|\fIinternal\fR|\fIexternal\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsensor-onboard\fR reads onboard sensors from a Raspberry Pi including
CPU temperature, GPU temperature, storage usage, and memory usage.
These sensors are useful for monitoring the health and performance of
WildlifeSystems nodes.
.PP
This script is typically invoked via the \fBsr\fR wrapper command rather
than directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B identify
Return exit code 60 to indicate this is a valid WildlifeSystems sensor script.
.TP
.B list
List all available onboard sensors, one per line. These are the names the
driver accepts as its argument.
.TP
.B mock
Emit fixed readings for every onboard sensor in the real output format, for
testing without the hardware and for \fBsr \-\-mock\fR. Works without
\fBpi-data\fR, using a fixed \fIsensor_id\fR prefix in its place.
.TP
.B setup
.TQ
.B enable
Nothing to do; print a note saying so and exit 0. Present so that every
driver answers the same commands.
.TP
.B \-\-version
.TQ
.B \-v
.TQ
.B version
Print the sensor-control package version, as the \fBsr\fR installed beside
\fBsensor-onboard\fR reports it, or \fIunknown\fR.
.TP
.B onboard_gpu
Read the GPU temperature in Celsius.
.TP
.B onboard_cpu
Read the CPU temperature in Celsius.
.TP
.B storage_used
Read storage usage as a percentage.
.TP
.B memory_used
Read memory usage as a percentage.
.TP
.B all
Read all available onboard sensors. This is the default with no argument.
.TP
.B internal
The same as \fBall\fR: every onboard sensor is internal.
.TP
.B external
Always an empty array, for the same reason.
.SH OUTPUT
A JSON array of readings in the WildlifeSystems format, produced by
.BR ws\-emit (1).
The CPU and GPU temperatures carry device \fIraspberry_pi\fR and location
\fI{{node}}\fR; \fIstorage_used\fR and \fImemory_used\fR measure nothing
physical, so they carry no device and location \fI{{none}}\fR. A reading that
could not be taken is emitted with \fIvalue\fR null and the reason in
\fIerror\fR, and the others are emitted as usual. A reading cannot be taken
when \fBvcgencmd\fR is unavailable or gives no single number for the GPU
temperature, when the kernel reports no CPU temperature, or when \fBdf\fR or
\fBfree\fR gives no usable figure for storage or memory use.
Where the node serial cannot be determined, \fIsensor_id\fR is null.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 20
Unrecognised argument, or \fBws\-emit\fR could not produce the readings, as
when \fBsc-prototype\fR is not installed.
.TP
.B 60
The \fBidentify\fR command, marking this as a WildlifeSystems driver.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBsensor-onboard list\fR
List available onboard sensors.
.TP
\fBsensor-onboard onboard_cpu\fR
Read the CPU temperature.
.TP
\fBsr onboard all\fR
Read all onboard sensors (recommended method).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sr (1),
.BR ws\-emit (1),
.BR sc-prototype (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Ed Baker <ed@ebaker.me.uk>
